---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why Rust — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Should I write the whole app in Rust, or put a web or Swift interface on top of a Rust engine? Here is what I weighed and what decided it."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why Rust</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Should I write the whole app in Rust, or put a web or Swift interface
          on top of a Rust engine? Here is what I weighed and what decided it.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I picked all Rust over a Rust engine with a React or Swift
          interface. I accepted a plainer interface and weaker accessibility.
          A photo editor sends each mouse movement to the engine 60 times a
          second. When two languages must stay in step that often, the bugs
          tend to show up in the handoff between them. Coding agents struggle
          with these bugs, so I would have to find them by hand.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="handoff-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="rust" aria-pressed="true">All Rust</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="split" aria-pressed="false">Rust engine + web UI</button>
          <label>Exposure <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" data-drag></label>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-run>Run 1 second</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 204" role="img" aria-label="One slider drag. The slider moves, the engine makes the edit, and new pixels appear. With all Rust, this is a function call with no runtime boundary. With a Rust engine and a web UI, each frame crosses the boundary between the two runtimes two times. Below, a row of 60 frames shows one second of drag.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE SLIDER DRAG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="26" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="46" text-anchor="middle" data-label="0">Slider moves</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="41" x2="214" y2="41"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 36 L224 41 L214 46 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="26" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="46" text-anchor="middle" data-label="1">Edit function</text>
