      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write down the trade-offs before you pick.</strong> The list
          showed me what I did not know yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick a design that you can debug yourself.</strong>
          Sometimes an agent fixes one symptom after another and does not find
          the cause.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plan for the next one or two steps.</strong> Swift is good
          for a Mac-only app, but I wanted a browser version, and that ruled
          Swift out right away.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Keep the core separate from the UI.</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why I considered Rust</h2>
      <p>
        I think that Rust is a very interesting language. It is roughly as
        powerful as C++, and its strict compiler refuses to build code that
        looks unsafe. It feels like a picky reviewer who sits next to me.
      </p>
      <p>
        But that does not make Rust the right choice for a photo editor. I had
        many questions:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Only the image engine in Rust, with a React UI?</li>
        <li>All of the app in Rust, if a Rust UI library is good enough?</li>
        <li>A Swift UI that feels like a native Mac app?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I also wanted a browser version one day, so that I can edit without
        my Mac. I might also want to support Windows and Linux one day.
      </p>

      <h2>The cost of each option</h2>
      <p>
        All Rust gives me one language. I do not copy image data back and
        forth between two programs, or pass each click and drag across a
        bridge between them. The app is faster, needs fewer external libraries, and is simpler to
        ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        But Rust has no official UI toolkit. The community libraries work, but
        my UI will not look as good as a well-styled web page or a native Mac
        app. Browsers give good accessibility (support for screen readers and
        similar tools) by default. The Rust UI libraries are far behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        A split design (for example, a React UI in Electron) lets me use the
        best tools on each side. With CSS, I control each pixel. The cost is
        two systems that I must keep in sync.
      </p>

      <h2>The Swift option</h2>
      <p>
        A Swift interface feels the most natural on a Mac. But it is also a
        split: Swift communicates with a Rust engine. Swift does not run in
        the browser, so a browser version (made with WebAssembly, which lets
        compiled code run in a web page) needs a second interface, written from
        scratch. Swift also ties me to Apple: Windows or Linux needs one more. The Rust UI libraries and Electron already run
        on all three.
      </p>

      <h2>Comparison</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">All Rust</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + React</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + Swift</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Languages</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1</td>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Engine and UI to keep in sync</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Extra runtime to ship</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">None</td>
              <td>Electron</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Appearance</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Plain</td>
              <td>Anything CSS can do</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Accessibility</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Limited</td>
              <td>Browser-grade</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browser (WASM) version</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Engine to WASM, UI as is</td>
              <td>Needs a second UI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Windows and Linux</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Same code</td>
              <td>Needs another UI</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Why the handoff decided it</h2>
      <p>
        In a web app, people expect a short delay. A photo editor is
        different. Drag, zoom, and pan are continuous. Each mouse movement must
        reach the engine and come back as a new image, with no lag that you
        can feel.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep two systems in step at that rate is much more difficult than
        to handle an occasional request. When they get out of step, the bug
        usually sits in the handoff, where the code on each side looks correct
        on its own. So this type of bug is very difficult to track down.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coding agents wrote most of this code. In my experience, they are
        strong when all of the code is in one language. They are much weaker
        when they must find a small mismatch between two systems in different
        languages. With one language and one program, there is no handoff
        where this bug can occur. When the agents get stuck, I can find the
        problem myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second reason was performance. I want the app to be fast and to
        use only the memory that it needs. All Rust has no second runtime to
        load, and no copies of the image go back and forth across a runtime
        boundary.
      </p>

      <h2>How I keep the option to change</h2>
      <p>
        I keep the UI and the photo editing core in separate modules, so I
        can swap the interface later. If I want a different interface in a
        year, most of the core comes with me without changes.
        For the same reason, I clean up sloppy generated code
        when it arrives. I want the code to stay small enough that I can
        understand all of it.
      </p>


